[{:db/id 17592186166930 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-affee19e-8bbb-5e06-bea1-1923b23ecbed" "question-69f45dd4-5ba1-5f14-94db-7e83a2055bbf"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166510 :question/problem "![](images/3f/3fa8db8d4186569020d5805ac76eee4f92f1c261dd2acb2502409a9b2b6db56b.svg)\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{\\text{[math]\\phantom{XXX}[/math]}}=-3{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ \\boxed{\\text{[math]\\phantom{XXX}[/math]}}=\\boxed{\\text{[math]\\phantom{XXXXXXXX}[/math]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{  x_1(t) \\right\\}$ and $X_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{  x_2(t) \\right\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t),$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-3{x}_{1}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{9{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=-3L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=9L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+5L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-3=-3{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-7=9{X}_{1}(s)+5{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{  x_1(t) \\right\\}$ and $X_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{  x_2(t) \\right\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t),$ respectively." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166931 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a9551a40-c332-54a5-8337-fb6b102278bb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166508 :question/problem "![](images/28/287223863402ec50768dee1bf0910226fbb0f9ee4818faaae4a0f1bd6121df26.svg)\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $\\mathcal{L}$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables $X_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\!\\left\\{  x_1(t) \\right\\}$ and $X_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\!\\left\\{  x_2(t) \\right\\}.$ \nFind the solution of the new system in terms of the parameter $s.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\,\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\,\\{-3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\,\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\,\\{3{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\,\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=-3L\\,\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+L\\,\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\,\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=3L\\,\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-L\\,\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-4=-3{X}_{1}(s)+{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-4=3{X}_{1}(s)-{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\!\\left\\{  x_1(t) \\right\\}$ and $X_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\!\\left\\{  x_2(t) \\right\\}.$\n\nRewriting the system in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(s+3){X}_{1}(s)-{X}_{2}(s)=4 \\\\ -3{X}_{1}(s)+(s+1){X}_{2}(s)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the first equation, we have\n\n$$\nX_2(s) = (s+3)X_1(s) - 4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{X}_{1}(s)+(s+1)\\,[(s+3){X}_{1}(s)-4] & =4 \\\\ -3{X}_{1}(s)+(s+1)(s+3){X}_{1}(s)-4(s+1) & =4 \\\\ [(s+1)(s+3)-3]{X}_{1}(s) & =4+4(s+1) \\\\ s(s+4){X}_{1}(s) & =4s+8 \\\\ {X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{4(s+2)}{s(s+4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find $X_2(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{2}(s) & =(s+3){X}_{1}(s)-4 \\\\  & =(s+3)\\,(\\frac{4(s+2)}{s(s+4)})-4 \\\\  & =\\frac{4(s+3)(s+2)-4s(s+4)}{s(s+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4({s}^{2}+5s+6)-4({s}^{2}+4s)}{s(s+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{s}^{2}+20s+24-4{s}^{2}-16s}{s(s+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(s+6)}{s(s+4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nX_1(s) =\\dfrac{4(s+2)}{s(s+4)}, \n\\qquad\nX_2(s) = \\dfrac{4(s+6)}{s(s+4)}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166509 :question/problem "![](images/8d/8da4fe8a52643867946a0818f3181fa57d6dc5b067155876e26689cb3fdde85e.svg)\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\nX_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ x_1(t) \\right\\} = \\dfrac{4}{s} + \\dfrac{1}{s-6}, \n\\qquad\nX_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ x_2(t) \\right\\} = \\dfrac{20}{s} - \\dfrac{1}{s-6}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem." :question/worked-solution "To find $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to $X_1(s)$ and $X_2(s){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{1}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{4}{s}+\\frac{1}{s-6}\\} \\\\  & =4{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\}+{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-6}\\} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1+{e}^{6\\cdot t} \\\\  & =4+{e}^{6t} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{2}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{20}{s}-\\frac{1}{s-6}\\} \\\\  & =20{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\}-{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-6}\\} \\\\  & =20\\cdot 1-{e}^{6\\cdot t} \\\\  & =20-{e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4+{e}^{6t} \\\\ 20-{e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-affee19e-8bbb-5e06-bea1-1923b23ecbed" :question/id #uuid "affee19e-8bbb-5e06-bea1-1923b23ecbed" :question/math-academy-id "q-331903" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-7{x}_{1}(t)-4{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-5{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{{field-1}}={{field-2}} \\\\ {{field-3}}=-5{X}_{1}(s)+4{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-7{x}_{1}(t)-4{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-5{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=-7L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-4L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=-5L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+4L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)+1=-7{X}_{1}(s)-4{X}_{2} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+6=-5{X}_{1}(s)+4{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8307f7fe-d121-59e4-a61b-10fda916ea42" "answer-field-e3eaffe6-2697-5bc4-b914-38bd0d4c4c37" "answer-field-bf6e9595-6b90-57b9-b075-5e46378b485a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8307f7fe-d121-59e4-a61b-10fda916ea42" :answer-field/id #uuid "8307f7fe-d121-59e4-a61b-10fda916ea42" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-458f3c5a-3381-501b-a7f2-dee6d946a149" :answer-field/choices ["answer-458f3c5a-3381-501b-a7f2-dee6d946a149" "a